{"text": "Marcus E. Delacroix, WSBA No. 48213\n\nDelacroix Injury Law, PLLC\n\n1201 Third Avenue, Suite 2950\n\nSeattle, WA 98101\n\nTelephone: (206) 555-0133\n\nFacsimile: (206) 555-0134\n\nmarcus@delacroixinjury.example\n\nAttorney for Plaintiffs\n\nUNITED STATES DISTRICT COURT\n\nWESTERN DISTRICT OF WASHINGTON AT SEATTLE\nANNELISE VAN DER BERG and JORDAN VAN DER BERG, a married couple,\n\nPlaintiffs,\n\nv.\n\nCASCADE RIDGE FREIGHT LINES, INC., a Delaware corporation; DWAYNE T.\nPRUETT, JR., an individual; and DOES 1-10,\n\nDefendants.\n\nCase No. 2:26-cv-00417-RSM\n\nCOMPLAINT FOR DAMAGES (Negligence; Negligent Hiring, Retention and\nEntrustment; Loss of Consortium)\n\nJURY DEMAND\n\nPlaintiffs allege as follows:\n\nI. PARTIES\n1. Plaintiff Annelise van der Berg (\"Ms. van der Berg\") is a lawful permanent\nresident of the United States and a citizen of the Netherlands who resides in\nBellingham, Whatcom County, Washington.\n\n2. Plaintiff Jordan van der Berg is Ms. van der Berg's husband. He resides with her\nin Bellingham and is a citizen of Washington.\n\n3. Defendant Cascade Ridge Freight Lines, Inc. (\"Cascade Ridge\") is a Delaware\ncorporation with its principal place of business at 8800 Industrial Parkway, Boise,\nIdaho 83716. It operates as an interstate motor carrier under USDOT No. 3184472.\nIts registered agent in Washington is Northwest Registered Agents of Olympia\nLLC.\n\n4. Defendant Dwayne T. Pruett, Jr. (\"Pruett\") is a resident of Nampa, Idaho, who at\nall relevant times held Idaho commercial driver's license no. TB184406K. At all\nrelevant times Pruett was employed by Cascade Ridge as a long-haul driver\n(employee no. CR-20871) and was acting within the course and scope of that\nemployment.\n\n5. Plaintiffs do not know the true names of the defendants sued as DOES 1-10 and\nwill amend this complaint when their identities are learned.\n\nII. JURISDICTION AND VENUE\n6. This Court has subject-matter jurisdiction under 28 U.S.C. § 1332(a) because the\nparties are citizens of different states or of a foreign state and the amount in\ncontroversy exceeds $75,000, exclusive of interest and costs.\n\n7. Venue is proper in this district under 28 U.S.C. § 1391(b)(2) because a substantial\npart of the events giving rise to the claims occurred in Skagit County, Washington.\n\nIII. FACTS\n8. On Thursday, October 9, 2025, at approximately 6:40 a.m., Ms. van der Berg was\ndriving her 2021 Subaru Outback (VIN 4S4BTANC8M3172905, Washington plate\nBXR4192) southbound on Interstate 5 near milepost 227 in Mount Vernon. She\nwas on her way to her job as a surgical nurse at Padilla Bay Medical Center.\nTraffic ahead had slowed for a disabled vehicle, and Ms. van der Berg slowed with\nit.\n\n9. Pruett was driving directly behind her in a 2019 Freightliner Cascadia tractor\n(VIN 3AKJHHDR7KSKF4418, Idaho plate A47 2291T) pulling a loaded 53-foot trailer\nowned by Cascade Ridge and identified as unit T5512.\n\n10. Pruett did not slow. The tractor struck the rear of the Subaru at highway\nspeed, drove it into the concrete median barrier, and pushed it roughly 140 feet\nbefore both vehicles came to rest.\n\n11. Washington State Patrol Trooper L. Okonjo (badge no. 1147) responded and\nprepared Collision Report No. E1298845. Trooper Okonjo cited Pruett under RCW\n46.61.145 for following too closely.\n\n12. Data from the tractor's electronic logging device (serial no. ELD7C20093318)\nshow that Pruett had been on duty for more than fifteen hours when the collision\noccurred, in violation of 49 C.F.R. § 395.3, and that the tractor was traveling 71\nmiles per hour four seconds before impact.\n\n13. Records for the cell phone Pruett carried that morning, number 208-555-0186,\nshow that he sent or received six text messages in the two minutes before the\ncollision.\n\n14. Before it hired Pruett in March 2024, Cascade Ridge knew or should have\nknown that he had a 2019 conviction for reckless driving in Canyon County, Idaho\n(Case No. CR14-19-08832), two earlier preventable collisions, and a license\nsuspension in 2021, and that he had failed a DOT drug screen while working for a\nprevious employer, Treasure Valley Hauling Co.\n\n15. Cascade Ridge's safety director, Rhonda Castellano, nonetheless approved\nPruett's application. In an email dated 3/18/2024, Ms. Castellano wrote that the\ncompany \"needs drivers\" and directed that Pruett be cleared without the road test\nrequired by the company's own written policy.\n\n16. Cascade Ridge dispatched Pruett on the morning of the collision knowing that\nhe could not lawfully complete the assigned run within his available hours of\nservice.\n\nIV. INJURIES AND DAMAGES\n17. Ms. van der Berg was taken by ambulance to the emergency department at\nPadilla Bay and was then airlifted to Harbor Heights Trauma Center in Seattle,\n\nwhere she remained an inpatient from October 9 until October 27, 2025 (medical\nrecord no. HH00672914).\n\n18. She sustained a burst fracture of the L1 vertebra, a traumatic brain injury, a\nfractured left femur and a splenic laceration. She underwent a spinal fusion\nperformed by Dr. Kenji Watanabe and a second surgery to repair the femur.\n\n19. Ms. van der Berg was fourteen weeks pregnant at the time of the collision. She\nlost the pregnancy three days later.\n\n20. She continues to suffer from chronic back pain, memory and concentration\ndeficits, and post-traumatic stress disorder, for which she is treated by\npsychologist Dr. Miriam Abelson. She has been prescribed sertraline 100 mg daily\nand oxycodone 5 mg as needed.\n\n21. Her medical expenses to date exceed $612,480.17, including $48,900 for air\ntransport. Her health insurer, Evergreen Mutual Health Plan (member ID\nEMH884201773; claim no. 2025CL0099417), asserts a subrogation interest of\n$377,204.66.\n\n22. Before the collision Ms. van der Berg earned approximately $118,300 per year.\nShe has not been able to return to work. Her future lost earnings are estimated at\nnot less than $1.4 million.\n\n23. Jordan is a self-employed boat builder who operates van der Berg Marine\nJoinery He closed his shop for four months to care for his wife and as a result lost\na contract, worth $86,000, to restore the schooner Marguerite. He has been\ndeprived of his wife's companionship, society and support.\n\n24. The Subaru was a total loss valued at $31,750. Plaintiffs' automobile carrier,\nCascadia Northwest Insurance Co. (policy no. PA7731440218), paid that amount\nless a $500 deductible.\n\nV. PRE-SUIT DEMAND\n25. On January 12, 2026, Plaintiffs' counsel sent a written demand to Cascade\nRidge's liability insurer, Great Basin Commercial Casualty (claim no.\nGBTRK25104482), to the attention of adjuster Paul Yoon\n(paul.yoon@greatbasincc.example; 1-800-555-0199 ext. 4471), for the policy limits\nof $1,000,000.\n\n26. On February 2, 2026, Mr. Yoon responded with an offer of $225,000, which\nPlaintiffs rejected.\n\nVI. FIRST CLAIM: NEGLIGENCE (against Pruett)\n27. Plaintiffs reallege the paragraphs above.\n\n28. Pruett owed Ms. van der Berg a duty to operate the tractor-trailer with\nreasonable care, to keep a proper lookout, to maintain a safe following distance,\nand to comply with state law and federal motor carrier safety regulations.\n\n29. Pruett breached those duties by following too closely, driving at an unsafe\nspeed, driving while fatigued and beyond his lawful hours of service, and using a\nhandheld phone while driving.\n\n30. Pruett's negligence was a proximate cause of the injuries and damages\ndescribed above.\n\nVII. SECOND CLAIM: VICARIOUS LIABILITY (against Cascade\nRidge)\n31. Plaintiffs reallege the paragraphs above.\n\n32. Pruett was acting within the course and scope of his employment when the\ncollision occurred. Cascade Ridge is liable for his negligence under the doctrine of\nrespondeat superior.\n\nVIII. THIRD CLAIM: NEGLIGENT HIRING, RETENTION,\nSUPERVISION AND ENTRUSTMENT (against Cascade Ridge)\n33. Plaintiffs reallege the paragraphs above.\n\n34. Cascade Ridge owed the motoring public a duty to use reasonable care in\nhiring, training, supervising and retaining its drivers and in entrusting\ncommercial vehicles to them.\n\n35. Cascade Ridge breached that duty by hiring and retaining Pruett despite his\nrecord, by waiving its own road test, by failing to monitor his hours of service,\nand by dispatching him on a run he could not lawfully complete.\n\n36. Cascade Ridge's negligence was a proximate cause of the injuries and damages\ndescribed above.\n\nIX. FOURTH CLAIM: LOSS OF CONSORTIUM (against all\nDefendants)\n37. Plaintiffs reallege the paragraphs above.\n\n38. As a direct result of Defendants' negligence, Jordan van der Berg has lost the\nlove, care, companionship and services of his wife, and will continue to suffer that\nloss.\n\nX. PRAYER FOR RELIEF\nPlaintiffs ask for judgment against Defendants, jointly and severally, as follows:\n\n special damages for past and future medical expenses, lost earnings and lost\nearning capacity, in amounts to be proved at trial;\n general damages for pain, suffering, disability, disfigurement and loss of\nenjoyment of life;\n damages for loss of consortium;\n property damage, prejudgment interest on liquidated sums, and taxable costs;\nand\n such other relief as the Court finds just.\n\nXI. JURY DEMAND\nUnder Fed. R. Civ. P. 38(b), Plaintiffs demand a trial by jury on all issues so triable.\n\nDATED this 19th day of February, 2026.\n\nDELACROIX INJURY LAW, PLLC\n\nBy: s/ Marcus E. Delacroix\n\nMarcus E. Delacroix, WSBA No. 48213\n\nAttorney for Plaintiffs\n\nVERIFICATION\nI, Annelise van der Berg, declare under penalty of perjury under the laws of the\nUnited States that I have read this complaint and that the facts stated in it are true\nto the best of my knowledge. Executed at Bellingham, Washington on February 18,\n2026.\n\n______________________________\n\nAnnelise van der Berg", "kind": "PDF", "detail": "7 pages · 1,528 words", "not